  1661	### 6.3 `race` — new job, two runners
  1662	
  1663	```yaml
  1664	  race:
  1665	    name: Race (${{ matrix.os }})
  1666	    runs-on: ${{ matrix.os }}
  1667	    strategy:
  1668	      fail-fast: false
  1669	      matrix:
  1670	        include:
  1671	          - os: macos-latest      # AC clause: go test -race ./... on the selected supported runner
  1672	            target: race-full
  1673	          - os: ubuntu-latest     # scope clause: race on at least Linux, named packages
  1674	            target: race
  1675	    steps:
  1676	      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
  1677	        with:
  1678	          submodules: true
  1679	
  1680	      - name: Checkout authoritative protocol suite
  1681	        uses: actions/checkout@v4
  1682	        with:
  1683	          repository: relux-works/curator-spec
  1684	          ref: ${{ env.SPEC_PIN }}
  1685	          path: protocol-spec
  1686	
  1687	      - uses: actions/setup-go@v5
  1688	        with:
  1689	          go-version-file: go.mod
  1690	
  1691	      - name: go test -race
  1692	        env:
  1693	          TOOLCHAIN_ALLOW_PATH: "1"          # hosted runner; see §6.2 note and §5.0
  1694	          CURATOR_CONFORMANCE_ROOT: ${{ github.workspace }}/protocol-spec/conformance/v1
  1695	        run: make ${{ matrix.target }}
  1696	```
  1697	
  1698	The job exports `CURATOR_CONFORMANCE_ROOT`; `make` picks it up through `PIN_ROOT ?=` and
  1699	`require-pin-root` fails the target if it is empty (§7). That is what makes the local `make race`
  1700	in §9.2 and this CI step the same gate — F3's fix.
  1701	
  1702	`windows-latest` is deliberately absent: the Go race detector on Windows requires a C toolchain and
  1703	this audit has no measurement that the hosted image satisfies it. If the producer measures that it
  1704	does, add the row; if not, **record the absence explicitly** rather than dropping it silently.
  1705	
  1706	**Named risk — this job is specified but unproven.** Two measurements bound it, and they disagree
  1707	about comfort rather than about facts:
  1708	
  1709	- `go test -count=1 -race ./...` at Go's **default** alarm: **exit 1**, `internal/install`
  1710	  FAIL 603.306 s, `internal/install/atomicity` FAIL 603.701 s (LOGBOOK 1637). This is what
  1711	  `-timeout 30m` exists to rescue.
  1712	- A **focused** 6-package race gate at `-timeout 45m`: **exit 0**, `internal/install`
  1713	  **609.117 s**, `internal/install/atomicity` **1422.407 s**
  1714	  (`.temp/TASK-260720-2284br/gates-rework1/gate-race.log`).
  1715	
  1716	So the packages do complete under race — atomicity at **1422.407 s against a 1800 s alarm, 1.27×
  1717	headroom**, measured with only five other packages in flight. The AC's gate runs the whole module,
  1718	where Go schedules up to `GOMAXPROCS` package binaries concurrently and per-package wall time rises.
  1719	**`go test -race ./... -timeout 30m` has never been executed.** `TASK-260720-jrrgw9` is back in
  1720	`development` carrying the test-only mitigation; `TASK-260729-3dr6hw` carries the production fix; and
  1721	atomicity's cost is byte-identical to the accepted worktree, so it is pre-existing debt rather than a
  1722	`jrrgw9` regression (LOGBOOK 1732).
  1723	
  1724	The producer must therefore treat step 3 of §9.1 as having a sibling: **measure `make race-full`
  1725	locally before writing this job**, and record per-package wall time, not just the exit code — the
  1726	number that matters is what atomicity does with the full module in flight. If it exceeds 1800 s, the
  1727	AC's `-race ./...` clause is blocked on `jrrgw9`/`3dr6hw`, not on 1pvfj5. Do not widen the timeout
  1728	past 30 m to make it pass — that was explicitly refused upstream ("no timeout override, assertion
  1729	deletion, source edit"). Report the real exit code either way.
